# 01-first-cell

The front matter of this document has `stuff: [a, b, c, d]`. The cell
below produces one bold paragraph per item:

**a**

**b**

**c**

**d**

# 02-metadata

The team has 3 members: ada, grace, linus. The lead is ADA.

This is version 12.1 (a number in the front matter; an inline cell
writes it the way `tostring` would).

Ada works at Analytical Engines. A key that may be absent is read with
`quarto.metadata.get`, which returns `nil` instead of raising: no phone
on file.

# 03-lists

## A tight list

Each item of a bullet list is a list of blocks, so an inline fragment is
wrapped in `pandoc.Plain`:

- Ada (lead)
- Grace (reviewer)

## Items with several blocks

A block fragment can hold any number of blocks, so an item can be more
than one paragraph:

- Ada is the lead.

  A second paragraph about Ada.

- Grace is the reviewer.

  A second paragraph about Grace.

## An ordered list

1.  Ada
2.  Grace

## A section per item

### ada

Member number 1.

### grace

Member number 2.

### linus

Member number 3.

# 04-shared-state

A cell with no `return` leaves nothing behind. Everything it defines is
visible to the cells after it:

## Hello, world

A [new]{.badge .text-bg-secondary} feature and a [beta]{.badge
.text-bg-secondary} one. The heading above got its id from its final
text.

# 05-fragment-functions

A function that returns a fragment is a reusable piece of markup. The
fragment here contains a block cell of its own, which splices `body` in,
so the outer cell needs a four-backtick fence:

::: {.card .p-3 .mb-3}
### Hello

Some *body* text.
:::

::: {.card .p-3 .mb-3}
### Bye

More.
:::

# 06-conditional

A cell that returns `nil` vanishes, so a condition around `return` is
all it takes:

::: {}
> **Warning**
>
> This is a draft of version 12.1.
:::

The same works per format:

You are not reading the HTML version.

# 07-walk

A fragment is a `pandoc.Blocks` value, so it has `walk`:

SOME *TEXT* TO SHOUT.

# 08-echo

With `--| echo: true` the cell shows the Lua it ran, fragments rewritten
into `pandoc` constructor calls, above its result:

``` {.lmd .code-with-copy}
return pandoc.Blocks({pandoc.Para({pandoc.Str("A"), pandoc.Space(), pandoc.Str("paragraph"), pandoc.Space(), pandoc.Str("with"), pandoc.Space(), pandoc.Emph({pandoc.Str("emphasis")}), pandoc.Str(","), pandoc.Space(), pandoc.Str("built"), pandoc.Space(), pandoc.Str("from"), pandoc.Space(), pandoc.Str("a"), pandoc.Space(), pandoc.Str("fragment.")})})
```

A paragraph with *emphasis*, built from a fragment.
